---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Does a static IP improve speed or gaming?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Not meaningfully: latency and bandwidth are routing properties, not address stability. Static addresses are about reachability, not performance." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Is a dynamic IP more private?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Marginally: your address changes, but tracking methods work around it, and the ISP still logs the assignment." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "How do I know which I have?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Reboot your router and compare: a changed public address means dynamic (our What Is My IP tool shows it before and after)." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Can a dynamic IP be blocklisted?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Yes, and you inherit it: check new assignments with the IP reputation checker before sending mail." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "What is a static lease?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "A DHCP reservation: your device always receives the same PRIVATE address from the router. Useful internally, unrelated to your public IP." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Do cloud servers have static IPs?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Cloud VMs typically keep their address for the instance's life; Elastic IPs (AWS) and reserved IPs make it explicit and portable." } }
  ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Static vs Dynamic IP Addresses"
  description="Static vs dynamic IP explained: how dynamic assignment works, when a static IP matters, and the workarounds when you cannot get one."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["IP & Networking", "/ip-and-networking/"], ["Static vs Dynamic IP"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ip-and-networking/" class="hover:text-accent">IP & Networking</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Static vs Dynamic IP</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">Static vs Dynamic IP Addresses</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">A static IP never changes; a dynamic IP is leased from a pool and can change at your provider's whim. For browsing, the difference is invisible. For hosting, mail, remote access, and VPNs, it is the difference between working and broken.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Static vs dynamic</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Static</th><th>Dynamic</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Changes</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Never</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Per lease</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Mail</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Clean PTR</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Pre-blocklisted</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Cost</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Paid</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Included</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#how-dynamic-assignment-works">How dynamic assignment works</a></li>
        <li><a href="#when-static-matters">When static matters</a></li>
        <li><a href="#the-workaround-when-static-is-unavailabl">The workaround when static is unavailable</a></li>
        <li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="how-dynamic-assignment-works">How dynamic assignment works</h2>
      <p>Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol (DHCP): your device asks the network for an address, and a DHCP server leases one from a pool for a set time. Renewals usually return the same address, but reboots, outages, and lease expirations can hand you a different one. Most consumer ISPs assign dynamic addresses; business lines and cloud providers offer static as standard or paid options.</p>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="when-static-matters">When static matters</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-ink">Self-hosting anything</strong> (website, mail, game server): DNS records and reverse DNS (PTR) assume a stable address.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Firewall allowlists:</strong> partners granting you access by IP will break on a dynamic address.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">VPN endpoints and remote access:</strong> predictable addressing is the point.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Email sending:</strong> dynamic space is blocklisted by policy on major lists (Spamhaus PBL exists for exactly this).</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="the-workaround-when-static-is-unavailabl">The workaround when static is unavailable</h2>
      <p>Dynamic DNS (DDNS): a small client updates a hostname’s DNS record whenever your address changes. It solves naming but not reputation: dynamic ranges still cannot send mail directly. For business needs, business-grade internet with a static IP (often a small monthly fee) is the clean answer.</p>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does a static IP improve speed or gaming?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Not meaningfully: latency and bandwidth are routing properties, not address stability. Static addresses are about reachability, not performance.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is a dynamic IP more private?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Marginally: your address changes, but tracking methods work around it, and the ISP still logs the assignment.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How do I know which I have?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Reboot your router and compare: a changed public address means dynamic (our What Is My IP tool shows it before and after).</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can a dynamic IP be blocklisted?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes, and you inherit it: check new assignments with the IP reputation checker before sending mail.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is a static lease?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">A DHCP reservation: your device always receives the same PRIVATE address from the router. Useful internally, unrelated to your public IP.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do cloud servers have static IPs?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Cloud VMs typically keep their address for the instance's life; Elastic IPs (AWS) and reserved IPs make it explicit and portable.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/my-ip/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">What Is My IP →</a></li><li><a href="/ptr-record/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">PTR records for mail →</a></li><li><a href="/ip-reputation-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">IP reputation checker →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
